# Android SDK Conventions

## Tech Stack

Kotlin, OpenTelemetry Android SDK, Gradle (multi-module), min API 21

## Project Structure

```
pulse-android-otel/
├── android-agent/           # Main agent (assembles all instrumentations)
├── core/                    # OpenTelemetry RUM core
├── common/                  # Shared utilities
├── services/                # Internal services (network, periodic work)
├── session/                 # Session management
├── pulse-android-sdk/       # Public API (PulseSDK)
├── pulse-android-sdk-internal/ # Internal SDK implementation details
├── pulse-semconv/           # Pulse semantic conventions (attribute keys)
├── pulse-utils/             # Utilities (toAttributes, toMap, etc.)
├── pulse-sampling/          # Sampling logic (models/, core/, remote/)
├── pulse-upload-plugin/     # Gradle plugin for source map + NDK obj uploads
├── instrumentation/         # Instrumentation modules (see below)
├── demo-app/                # Demo application
├── test-common/             # Shared test utilities
└── buildSrc/                # Shared Gradle convention plugins
```

## Instrumentation Modules

All instrumentations live under `instrumentation/`. Two layout patterns:

**Flat modules** (simple, single `build.gradle.kts`):
`activity/`, `fragment/`, `anr/`, `crash/`, `network/`, `slowrendering/`, `sessions/`, `startup/`, `view-click/`, `compose/click/`

**Multi-submodule** (complex instrumentations):

| Module | Submodules | Pattern |
|--------|------------|---------|
| `okhttp3/`, `okhttp3-websocket/`, `httpurlconnection/`, `android-log/` | `library/`, `agent/`, `testing/` | Runtime / build-time / tests |
| `interaction/` | `core/`, `library/`, `remote/` | Business logic / OTel integration / API client |
| `location/` | `core/`, `library/`, `models/`, `processors/` | Provider / OTel integration / data / attribute appenders |

New instrumentations should follow the flat pattern unless they need build-time bytecode instrumentation (use `library/agent/testing`) or have distinct core logic and API clients (use `core/library/remote`).

## Package Naming

Two package roots — never mix them:

- `io.opentelemetry.android.*` — OTel-upstream code (instrumentations, session, internal services)
- `com.pulse.*` — Pulse-specific code (SDK API, semantic conventions, sampling, utilities)

## Gradle Conventions

- All library modules apply `otel.android-library-conventions` and `otel.publish-conventions` from `buildSrc/`
- `settings.gradle.kts` uses `includeFromDir("instrumentation")` to auto-discover modules up to 3 levels deep
- Typical module depends on `projects.instrumentation.commonApi`, `projects.instrumentation.androidInstrumentation`, `projects.services`, `projects.session`, `projects.common`

## Pulse upload plugin (`pulse-upload-plugin`)

Apply `id("pulse.plugin")` on app modules. Both tasks use the same `PulseUploadSourceMapsTask` implementation with different `PulseSymbolUploadKind`:

| Task | Upload |
|---|---|
| `uploadSourceMaps` | ProGuard/R8 `mapping.txt` (`type=mapping`) |
| `uploadSymbolFiles` | Unstripped NDK `.so` from `pulse.symbols.objFile` (`type=ndk`; zip also supported) |

Configure via `pulse { sourcemaps { ... } symbols { objFile.set(...) } }`. Symbol upload depends on `externalNativeBuild*` tasks.

## Use libs.versions.toml for dependencies

Do not hardcode dependency coordinates or plugin versions in Gradle files.

### Discouraged

```kotlin
id("net.bytebuddy.byte-buddy-gradle-plugin") version "1.17.8"

implementation("org.dreamhorizon.instrumentation:okhttp3-library")
byteBuddy("org.dreamhorizon.instrumentation:okhttp3-agent")
```

### Preferred

```kotlin
alias(libs.plugins.bytebuddy)

implementation(libs.okhttp3.library)
byteBuddy(libs.okhttp3.agent)
```

### Catalog placement rules
- SDK/shared dependencies → <root_project>/gradle/libs.versions.toml
- Demo-app-only dependencies → demo-app/gradle/libs.versions.toml
- Do not place demo-app-only dependencies in the root catalog
- When a dependency already exists in the root catalog, access it from demo-app using and do not duplicate the same dependency entry in demo-app/gradle/libs.versions.toml.
  ```kotlin
  rootLibs.some.library
  ```
- When adding a new dependency and version entry, place it near similar/group-related dependencies in libs.versions.toml

## File & Class Naming

| Type | Convention | Examples |
|------|------------|---------|
| Instrumentation classes | `*Instrumentation` | `ActivityLifecycleInstrumentation`, `OkHttpInstrumentation` |
| Support classes | `*Tracker`, `*Tracer`, `*Listener`, `*Manager`, `*Provider`, `*Appender`, `*Extractor` | `SessionManager`, `ActivityTracer`, `SlowRenderListener` |
| Test classes | `*Test.kt` | `InteractionManagerTest.kt` |
| File naming | One top-level class per file; filename matches class name | `SessionManager.kt` → `class SessionManager` |

## Discovery & Registration

All instrumentations use `@AutoService(AndroidInstrumentation::class)` for automatic discovery — no manual registration needed.

## Visibility

- `public` for SDK API surface (`PulseSDK`, `AndroidInstrumentation` interface)
- `internal` for module-internal code that should not leak to consumers
- `@JvmOverloads` / `@JvmStatic` where Java interop is needed

## Extension Functions

Shared extensions live in `pulse-utils` (`com.pulse.otel.utils`):
- `Map<String, Any?>.toAttributes()` / `Attributes.toMap()`
- `AttributesBuilder putAttributesFrom Map` (infix)

## Companion Object Patterns

- Constants: `companion object { const val INSTRUMENTATION_NAME = "..." }`
- Singletons: `PulseSDK.INSTANCE` via `by lazy`
- Factory methods: `NetworkDetector.create(context)`

## Span Types (`pulse.type`)

- `interaction` — critical user interaction spans
- `screen_session` / `screen_load` — screen tracking
- `app_start` — cold/warm start
- `device.anr` / `device.crash` / `non_fatal` — stability events
- `app.jank.frozen` / `app.jank.slow` — rendering performance
- `network.<status>` / `network.change` — network calls and connectivity
- `session.start` / `session.end` — session boundaries
- `custom_event` — developer-defined events
- `app.click` — touch/click events

## Span Attributes

Use `pulse.*` prefixed attributes for Pulse-specific data:
- `pulse.type` — categorizes the span (see list above)
- `pulse.interaction.name` — critical interaction name
- `pulse.interaction.id`, `pulse.interaction.apdex_score`, `pulse.interaction.user_category`
- `screen.name`, `last.screen.name` — screen identifiers
- `app.interaction.frozen_frame_count`, `app.interaction.slow_frame_count` — rendering metrics
- `pulse.session.anr.count`, `pulse.session.crash.count` — session-level stability counters

## Resource Attributes

- `app.build_name` — app version (materialized as `AppVersion` in ClickHouse)
- `os.name` — platform (materialized as `Platform`)
- `os.version` — OS version (materialized as `OsVersion`)
- `device.model.name` — device model (materialized as `DeviceModel`)
- `rum.sdk.version` — SDK version

## Concurrency

**Always prefer Kotlin coroutines over Java executors for new Pulse-owned code.**

| Situation | Use |
|---|---|
| Background / periodic work in Pulse-specific modules | `CoroutineScope + launch + delay` |
| One-shot async in Pulse modules | `scope.launch(ioDispatcher) { ... }` |
| Existing upstream OTel Android code (`services/`, `core/`) | Keep existing `ThreadPoolExecutor` / `Handler` — do NOT refactor |

- Inject `CoroutineScope` and `CoroutineDispatcher` as constructor parameters so they can be swapped in tests (use `TestCoroutineScheduler` / `UnconfinedTestDispatcher`)
- **Never** introduce `ScheduledExecutorService` or `Handler.postDelayed` in new Pulse instrumentation code
- The `services/PeriodicWorkImpl.kt` (`ThreadPoolExecutor` + `Handler`) is upstream OTel Android code — do **not** copy that pattern into new Pulse modules; it exists only for OTel compatibility
- Coroutine dependency: `kotlinx-coroutines-android` is already in `libs.versions.toml`

## Coroutine: Do not hardcode Dispatchers

Avoid direct usage of `Dispatchers.IO`, `Dispatchers.Default`, `Dispatchers.Main` in production code. So that when testing this class we can replace Dispatchers with StandardTestDispatchers

### Discouraged

```kotlin
class UtilityClass {
    withContext(Dispatchers.IO) { }
    launch(Dispatchers.Default) { }
}
```

### Preferred
```kotlin
class UtilityClass(
    val io: CoroutineDispatcher,
    val default: CoroutineDispatcher,
    val main: CoroutineDispatcher
) {
    withContext(io) { }
    launch(default) { }
}
```


## Imports

- **No wildcard imports** — `import foo.*` is forbidden
- **No fully qualified names (FQN) in code** — add a top-level `import` and use the short name

FQN references in expressions, declarations, or type positions are not allowed. Examples of forbidden patterns:

- `kotlinx.coroutines.flow.MutableStateFlow(...)`
- `io.opentelemetry.android.internal.services.visiblescreen.VisibleScreenState(...)`

```kotlin
// ❌ BAD — FQN in code body
every { tracker.visibleScreenState } returns
    kotlinx.coroutines.flow.MutableStateFlow(
        io.opentelemetry.android.internal.services.visiblescreen.VisibleScreenState(
            screenName = "unknown",
        ),
    )

// ✅ GOOD — import + short names
import kotlinx.coroutines.flow.MutableStateFlow
import io.opentelemetry.android.internal.services.visiblescreen.VisibleScreenState

every { tracker.visibleScreenState } returns
    MutableStateFlow(VisibleScreenState(screenName = "unknown"))
```

**Allowed:** `import` lines, `package` declarations, annotation class literals (e.g. `@AutoService(Foo::class)`).

**Name clashes:** use `import foo.Bar as FooBar` — never fall back to FQN in the body.

## Localize Kotlin suppressions

Avoid applying `@Suppress(...)` to entire functions, classes, files, or large scopes when the warning originates from a single statement.

Prefer the suppression to be placed directly on the minimal expression or statement that requires it.

### Discouraged

```kotlin
@Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
private fun ReadWriteLogRecord.setAllAttributesIfAbsent(attributes: Attributes) {
    attributes.asMap().forEach { (key, value) ->
        if (this.attributes[key] == null) {
            setAttribute(key as AttributeKey<Any>, value as Any)
        }
    }
}
```

### Preferred

```kotlin
private fun ReadWriteLogRecord.setAllAttributesIfAbsent(attributes: Attributes) {
    attributes.asMap().forEach { (key, value) ->
        if (this.attributes[key] == null) {
            @Suppress("UNCHECKED_CAST")
            setAttribute(key as AttributeKey<Any>, value as Any)
        }
    }
}
```

## Public API: avoid data classes

Do not expose `data class` types in public APIs.

### Discouraged

```kotlin
data class UserResponse(
    val id: String,
    val name: String
)
```

### Preferred

```kotlin
interface User {
    val id: String
    val name: String
}
```
or

```kotlin
class User internal constructor(
    val id: String,
    val name: String
)
```

Below is allowed

```kotlin
internal data class User(
    val id: String,
    val name: String
)
```

## Do not hide failures with fallback objects

Avoid returning empty/default objects from parsing, deserialization, mapping, or conversion failures.

Prefer returning `null` (or `Result`) so failure is explicit and callers must handle it.

### Discouraged

```kotlin
fun fromJsonString(json: String?): PulseAppMetaData {
    if (json.isNullOrBlank()) {
        return PulseAppMetaData()
    }

    return runCatching {
        json.fromJson<PulseAppMetaData>(TAG) ?: PulseAppMetaData()
    }.getOrElse { PulseAppMetaData() }
}
```

### Preferred
```kotlin
fun fromJsonString(json: String?): PulseAppMetaData? {
    if (json.isNullOrBlank()) return null

    return runCatching {
        json.fromJson<PulseAppMetaData>(TAG)
    }.getOrNull()
}
```

## Prefer `apply` in builder-style extension functions

 For mutating extension functions that return `this`, prefer expression bodies with `apply`.

### Discouraged

 ```kotlin
private fun AttributesBuilder.putMetaData(
    crashMetaData: PulseAppMetaData
): AttributesBuilder {
    // mutations
    return this
}
 ```

 ### Preferred

 ```kotlin
private fun AttributesBuilder.putMetaData(
    crashMetaData: PulseAppMetaData
): AttributesBuilder = apply {
    // mutations
}
 ```

## Best Practices

- Group imports by source (stdlib, Android, OTel, project)
- Prefer `internal` for non-API classes
- Use `@AutoService` for all new instrumentations
- Keep one top-level class per file (test helper classes are an exception)

## Publishing

Automated via `.github/workflows/publish-android.yml`

## Code Style

Follow Kotlin conventions — `camelCase` for functions/properties, `PascalCase` for classes. Formatting enforced by Spotless (`otel.spotless-conventions`).

## Minimal Diffs — No Cosmetic Changes

When editing any file, **only change what is required by the task**. Never introduce:

- Indentation reformatting on untouched lines
- Brace-style conversions (e.g. `if (x) { return }` → `if (x) return`)
- Comment removal or rewording on lines you are not otherwise modifying
- Blank-line addition or removal outside the changed block
- Import reordering unrelated to added/removed symbols

If a task requires rewriting a whole function or class, preserve the exact formatting, brace style, blank lines, and comments of every line that is **not** part of the functional change. When in doubt, use a targeted `StrReplace` on the minimal affected lines rather than rewriting the whole block.

## File Headers

**Do NOT add any copyright/license header to new Pulse-authored files.**

Only pre-existing files that were forked or copied from the upstream OpenTelemetry Android project carry the OTel Apache-2.0 header:

```
/*
 * Copyright The OpenTelemetry Authors
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 */
```

New files created by the Pulse team — even when placed under `io.opentelemetry.android.*` packages — must have **no header at all**; the package declaration is the first line.
